   $q$Walking times for seven students (minutes): 5, 6, 7, 7, 8, 9, 30. What happens to the mean and the median if the time of 30 minutes is removed?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The mean stays the same and the median drops to 6.5 minutes","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-outlier-effect"},{"text":"The mean and the median both drop by about 3.3 minutes","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-median-shifts-like-mean"},{"text":"The mean drops by 23 minutes and the median stays 7 minutes","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"subtracted-outlier-from-mean"},{"text":"The mean drops by about 3.3 minutes and the median stays 7 minutes","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The mean drops by about 3.3 minutes and the median stays 7 minutes$q$, $q$The mean goes from $72 \div 7 \approx 10.3$ to $42 \div 6 = 7$, a drop of about 3.3 minutes, while the median of 5, 6, 7, 7, 8, 9 is still $\frac{7 + 7}{2} = 7$. The median barely reacts to one extreme value, so it does not drop along with the mean.$q$, 'draft', null)
